From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Sat, 29 Aug 2026 21:44:17 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 10331/11362] Make the next unexplained process death
 self-describing (REQ-INFRA-6830)

A 7-hour GPU A/B died at 38 of 39 cells on 2026-08-29 and nothing in this
repository could say what killed it: the llama-server log stops mid-decode at
23.37 tok/s with no shutdown line, the runner log stops with no traceback, there
is no OOM trace, and run_stop_authority.py -- which logs EVERY action it takes --
wrote no actor row. Enough to conclude it was killed from outside; not enough to
say by whom. This project already names that state: "exit-143, sender never
identified", first recorded 2026-08-09.

I nearly recorded a third wrong cause. My first read blamed kill_gpu_zombies
because its comments describe this exact symptom. But llama-server is in that
reaper's exemption list, run_stop_authority would have logged, and there are
three reapers with three exemption lists -- picking the one whose comments best
match is how a guess becomes fact. I had already made that error today with a
GPU-contention story the base rate refuted. So this claims no culprit.

It makes the next death self-describing instead: signal number and name, pid and
ppid, elapsed time, and how far the work had got -- "killed at 38/39" rather than
"killed". Naming the sender is the precondition for fixing whatever kills, and
this project has twice skipped that step.

The handler re-raises the default action rather than exiting, so a job never
becomes harder to stop than before -- swallowing SIGTERM turns a polite stop into
a hang. SIGKILL is deliberately uncaught: a job that vanishes with NO receipt is
then itself evidence of SIGKILL, OOM, or a kernel event, and that absence is only
informative because a catchable signal reliably leaves one. Pinned by a test so
nobody later "fixes" the gap.

6 tests in real child processes, because the behaviour under test is death.
5/5 mutations RED; restores byte-identical.

